1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:35,000
When I start a small tool, the first question is what someone can finish with it. A long feature list is easy to write. A useful first result takes more care. For a reading tool, that result might be a page that connects one important idea to the source passage. The first release should make that path work from beginning to end.

2
00:00:35,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Consider a person who has a transcript but no time to read it all. They need the main argument, the evidence behind it, and any conditions that change the conclusion. Giving them only headings makes them return to the original for every detail. Hiding every qualification in an expandable panel can make the main page sound more certain than the speaker was.

3
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:45,000
A small example is useful when it shows both the result and the path that produced it. Include an input, a command, and the resulting file. Say what has actually been tested. A sample built from supplied text can prove that the page renderer works, but it cannot prove that downloading a podcast or recognizing difficult audio will succeed.

4
00:01:45,000 --> 00:02:20,000
I prefer to add features after observing a repeated problem. If several readers cannot find the right passage, improve navigation. If they cannot understand a claim, improve the explanation. Building an account system first would not answer either question. This is a preference for a small personal tool, not a rule that every regulated or collaborative product should follow.

5
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The first successful release is not the end of the work. Ask a new reader to use it without a live explanation. Watch where they stop and record the limitation honestly. A clear boundary is more useful than an impressive promise. Once the path is reliable, the next improvement can respond to an actual need instead of an imagined one.
